                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "e30adff5-b49d-37d6-a23b-d45abdb572e0",
                        "entry_id": "11fa8a7b-b059-3fd0-9ab3-ddcc2244db52",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Untoward."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "67c7624a-2b1b-4179-90d7-8efc3eb3421a",
                                "sense_id": "e30adff5-b49d-37d6-a23b-d45abdb572e0",
                                "text": "1561, Baldassare Castiglione, The Book of the Courtier (1528), translated by Thomas Hoby, London: David Nutt, 1900, Book One, pp. 41-42,\nTo disgrace therefore many untowardly asseheades, that through malepertnes thinke to purchase them the name of a good Courtyer, I would have suche a pastime for this night, that one of the company myght bee picked out who should take in hand to shape in woordes a good Courtyer, specifying all suche condicions and particuler qualities, as of necessitie must be in hym that deserveth this name."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "498c4ace-3f3e-4e19-87e7-e74dfd4996b1",
                                "sense_id": "e30adff5-b49d-37d6-a23b-d45abdb572e0",
                                "text": "They frequently learn from unbred or debauched Servants, such Language, untowardly Tricks and Vices, as otherwise they possibly would be ignorant of all their Lives."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "1c8f0670-5581-4854-afa7-c3ad1ed9bb33",
                                "sense_id": "e30adff5-b49d-37d6-a23b-d45abdb572e0",
                                "text": "1791, George Washington, Letter to his niece, Harriot Washington, Philadelphia, 20 October, 1791,\nYour cousins, with whom you live, are well qualified to give you advice; and I am sure they will, if you are disposed to receive it. But, if you are disobliging, self-willed, and untowardly, it is hardly to be expected that they will engage themselves in unpleasant disputes with you […]"
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
